[1mScope.[22m The project is a single function: [38;5;10mvoid normalize(float *dest, const float *src, int size)[0m. [38;5;10mtranslation/Cargo.toml[0m has no [38;5;10m[features][0m section, so there is exactly one valid feature combination (the empty set). [38;5;10mCMakeLists.txt[0m has no options or conditional compilation either — one C configuration, linking [38;5;10mlibm[0m. I verified both debug and release Rust profiles, and additionally against a [38;5;10m-O3[0m C build, since [38;5;10mpanic = "abort"[0m plus optimization is where float codegen could plausibly diverge.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWhat I added[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mtranslation/tests/differential.rs[0m — loads the C [38;5;10m.so[0m and the Rust [38;5;10mcdylib[0m via [38;5;10mlibloading[0m and calls [38;5;10mnormalize[0m only through the exported symbol, never directly. Comparisons are on [38;5;10mf32[0m bit patterns, so [38;5;10m-0.0[0m vs [38;5;10m0.0[0m and differing NaN payloads count as mismatches. Each input is exercised three ways: separate buffers, in-place ([38;5;10mdest == src[0m), and partially overlapping ([38;5;10mdest[0m trailing [38;5;10msrc[0m in one allocation, which the C code handles element-wise).[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mverify.sh[0m — enumerates feature subsets from [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m, builds the C reference, then per combination runs [38;5;10mcargo check[0m, builds both profiles, diffs exported symbols, and runs the tests.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mlibloading = "0.8"[0m under [38;5;10m[dev-dependencies][0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mSymbol parity.[22m The C [38;5;10m.so[0m exports exactly one symbol, [38;5;10mnormalize[0m; the Rust [38;5;10mcdylib[0m exports it under the same name. No missing exports.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mBehaviours confirmed identical[22m, including the unusual ones the C code implies: a NaN anywhere makes [38;5;10msum > 0.0f[0m false, so the [38;5;10mmemset[0m branch runs rather than the scaling branch; subnormal inputs square to zero, so a non-zero input can still be zeroed; [38;5;10msum[0m overflowing to infinity makes the reciprocal zero, turning finite entries into signed zeros and infinite entries into NaN; [38;5;10msize == 0[0m leaves [38;5;10mdest[0m untouched because the [38;5;10mmemset[0m length is zero; and negative [38;5;10msize[0m with [38;5;10mdest == src[0m writes nothing. I did not exercise negative [38;5;10msize[0m with distinct buffers — [38;5;10msize * sizeof(float)[0m sign-extends into a huge [38;5;10mmemset[0m length and crashes in C as well, so there is no defined behaviour to match.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne harness defect worth flagging.[22m [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does not rebuild a [38;5;10mcrate-type = ["cdylib"][0m target, so the tests initially loaded a stale [38;5;10m.so[0m and passed even against deliberately broken code. I caught this by mutation-testing and added a staleness guard that fails if the [38;5;10m.so[0m predates any file in [38;5;10msrc/[0m. After that fix, three mutations — [38;5;10mf64[0m accumulation, [38;5;10mf64[0m [38;5;10msqrt[0m, and dropping the [38;5;10mdest != src[0m check — were each detected. [38;5;10mverify.sh[0m also builds before testing.[0m[0m
